WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.547
Thirty-eight people watched.

00:00:01.557 --> 00:00:03.037
Or so the story says.

00:00:03.047 --> 00:00:06.016
In 1964, The New York Times said

00:00:06.026 --> 00:00:08.966
thirty-eight neighbours saw
or heard Kitty Genovese's

00:00:08.976 --> 00:00:11.021
murder, and nobody helped.

00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:12.977
It became the textbook example of the

00:00:12.987 --> 00:00:14.436
bystander effect.

00:00:14.446 --> 00:00:17.086
Lab studies do find people less likely

00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:18.919
to help when others are around.

00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:20.856
But the story fell apart.

00:00:20.881 --> 00:00:24.396
In 2007, researchers found no evidence of

00:00:24.406 --> 00:00:26.746
thirty-eight witnesses who saw it and did

00:00:26.756 --> 00:00:27.527
nothing.

00:00:27.537 --> 00:00:30.052
In 2016, the Times itself called its

00:00:30.062 --> 00:00:31.216
story flawed.

00:00:31.226 --> 00:00:32.601
So what happens?

00:00:32.614 --> 00:00:35.279
Researchers studied two
hundred and nineteen real

00:00:35.289 --> 00:00:37.903
street conflicts on security cameras.

00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:39.878
In nine out of ten, at least

00:00:39.888 --> 00:00:42.603
one bystander did
something to help, usually

00:00:42.613 --> 00:00:43.420
several.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.170
More people meant a better chance someone

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.020
helped.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.805
So, did thirty-eight really just watch?
